## The Top 10 XIOM Pitfalls

### 1. Semicolon on Tail Expression

```xiom
// WRONG — semicolon discards the value!
fn add(a: Int, b: Int) -> Int {
  a + b;  // Type error: expected Int, got Unit
}

// RIGHT — no semicolon
fn add(a: Int, b: Int) -> Int {
  a + b
}
```

### 2. Using = Instead of ==

```xiom
let x = 5;
// if x = 5 { ... }  // WRONG: this is assignment, not comparison!
if x == 5 { ... }    // RIGHT: comparison
```

### 3. Integer Division Surprise

```xiom
let result = 5 / 2;        // 2, NOT 2.5!
let correct = 5.0 / 2.0;   // 2.5
let also_correct = 5 as Float64 / 2 as Float64;  // 2.5
```

### 4. Forgetting `use` for Modules

```xiom
// WRONG — string functions not found!
// let s = string.str_concat("a", "b");

// RIGHT
use xiom.string;
let s = string.str_concat("a", "b");
```

### 5. Mismatched Return Types

```xiom
// WRONG
fn get_value(flag: Bool) -> Int {
  if flag { return 42; }
  // Missing return for when flag is false!
}

// RIGHT — every path returns an Int
fn get_value(flag: Bool) -> Int {
  if flag { 42 } else { 0 }
}
```

### 6. Reassigning let

```xiom
let x = 10;
// x = 20;  // ERROR: let is immutable!
var y = 10;
y = 20;     // OK
```

### 7. Off-by-One Errors

```xiom
// Range 0..5 gives 0,1,2,3,4 (NOT 5)
var count = 0;
for i in 0..5 { count += 1; }  // count is 5
i
// Use 0..6 to include 5 in counting to 5
```

### 8. Infinite Loop

```xiom
// WRONG — counter never changes!
var i = 0;
while i < 10 {
  io.println("Forever...");
  // Forgot i += 1!
}

// RIGHT
var i = 0;
while i < 10 {
  io.println("Looping...");
  i += 1;
}
```

### 9. Uninitialized var Usage

```xiom
// Always initialize var when declaring
var score = 0;  // GOOD
// var badge;   // Avoid — uninitialized
```

### 10. Confusing Str and Char Quotes

```xiom
let s: Str = "A";  // Str — double quotes
let c: Char = 'A'; // Char — single quotes
```

